1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,143
Vericoding is a way of writing software
where the specification, the proof, and
the program are all one artefact, checked
by a machine.

2
00:00:10,067 --> 00:00:11,395
Normally we test.

3
00:00:11,395 --> 00:00:17,335
A test runs the program on a handful of
inputs, and we hope the rest behave.

4
00:00:17,335 --> 00:00:19,602
A specification is different.

5
00:00:19,602 --> 00:00:27,261
It says something about every possible
input, and a proof is a certificate that
the code meets it.

6
00:00:27,261 --> 00:00:28,277
Tests sample.

7
00:00:28,277 --> 00:00:29,528
Proofs quantify.

8
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:34,183
So a vericoded program is three things at
once.

9
00:00:34,183 --> 00:00:34,863
The code.

10
00:00:34,863 --> 00:00:37,883
A statement of what that code must mean.

11
00:00:37,883 --> 00:00:43,170
And a proof, checked by the kernel, that
the code meets the statement.

12
00:00:43,170 --> 00:00:50,042
One language for all three, so the
specification cannot drift away from the
implementation.

13
00:00:51,067 --> 00:00:51,587
Why now?

14
00:00:51,587 --> 00:00:53,798
Proving used to be the bottleneck.

15
00:00:53,798 --> 00:01:01,211
A theorem prover could check everything,
but writing the proofs took specialists
far longer than writing the code.

16
00:01:01,211 --> 00:01:04,072
Large language models changed the
economics.

17
00:01:04,072 --> 00:01:09,599
Routine obligations get discharged, lemmas
get suggested, broken proofs get repaired.

18
00:01:09,599 --> 00:01:12,330
The proof is no longer the expensive part.

19
00:01:13,333 --> 00:01:15,522
That moves the scarce resource.

20
00:01:15,522 --> 00:01:19,334
The machine cannot invent what your
program must mean.

21
00:01:19,334 --> 00:01:27,946
You iterate at the specification surface,
in conversation, and the implementation
becomes something you can replace later.

22
00:01:27,946 --> 00:01:31,123
The dialectic is about intent, not
operation.

23
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:35,085
Next, why a proof is, literally, a
program.

